A lifetime under a loop over a const gets one schedule per copy, as it does
under a loop over state.

What the loop iterates says how many copies there are, not that they may share
state. `timer`'s override is a component with a handle of its own, so under
any `for` it is a runtime instance, and a loop holding one is a render slot
whether its iterable can change or not: the slot is where a target keeps the
list of live instances, one handle each. The second loop has a reactive `if`
inside it, which as a render func of its own read a loop variable only the host
loop bound (`undefined: p`).

Both used to be refused -- "this loop is not reactive and "timer" brackets a
lifetime" -- because a const loop spliced the body once and hoisted its handle
once, so the second mount overwrote the first and that schedule could never be
stopped. And an instance's @unmount was the program's exit handler: fyne's
`main` called `m.__snglTeardown` on a Model that has none, since the timer's
teardown is the instance's own destroy.

deny out/go/fyne/model.go `m\.__n\d+ = newTimerInstance` -- a copy is kept in the slot's instance list, not in one Model field each copy overwrites
deny out/go/gtk4/model.go `m\.__n\d+ = newTimerInstance` -- a copy is kept in the slot's instance list, not in one Model field each copy overwrites

-- app.sngl --
import ui "sngl:ui"
import time "sngl:time"

output {
    go {
        bubbletea
        fyne
        gtk4
    }
    none {
        html
    }
}

var n = 0
var shown = true
const periods list<time.duration> = [100ms, 250ms]

ui.window(title="t") {
    for var p = periods {
        time.timer(interval=p, enabled=true, @tick { n = n + 1 })
    }
    for var p = periods {
        if shown {
            time.timer(interval=p, enabled=true, @tick { n = n + 1 })
        }
    }
    ui.button(text="hide", @click { shown = false })
    ui.text(value=string(n))
}
